//! image/lasso_summary.rye -- read a query POLYGON (a free-form LASSO) as a small DIGEST TABLE: join image/lasso_partition.rye's three
//! window-versus-crossing classes (CONTAINED, CLIPPED, OUTSIDE) with image/scene_manifest.rye's per-object facts (area, holes) into one
//! struct a surface reads to fill a lasso status line -- how many objects a window select keeps, how many a crossing select would add,
//! how much foreground each class carries, and how many holed objects sit in each. Exact integers, NO float and NO tolerance.
//!
//! Season G (Open Media Primitives) -- this is the FREE-FORM companion of image/region_summary.rye, and the digest companion of
//! image/lasso_partition.rye. region_summary read a RECTANGLE query as a digest; lasso_partition split a POLYGON's crossing set into the
//! objects it holds WHOLE and the objects it holds only in PART, reading the three class SIZES. This rung reads the three class FACTS over
//! a polygon: `lasso_summary` walks the manifest once, classifies each object by the even-odd PIXEL arbiter (not a box test), and folds
//! each object's whole-scene area and hole count into its class, then `largest_contained` names the biggest object a window select keeps.
//! It composes over lasso_partition for the containment set, over lasso_select for the crossing set, and over scene_manifest for the
//! per-object area and holes -- a reading over readings, no plane of its own, no pixel written.
//!
//! The DECISIVE crux -- the box-is-a-prefilter lesson lifted from the OBJECT to its AREA, one shape freer than region_summary, where the
//! concavity now moves AREA between classes. region_summary classified each object by the tight bounding box, which for a RECTANGLE is
//! the EXACT arbiter of containment, so its `contained_area` was exact by construction. For a CONCAVE LASSO that box test BREAKS: a
//! polygon's slot can carve pixels out of the middle of an object whose whole bounding box -- and both box corners -- lie inside the loop,
//! so a box-based digest would call that object CONTAINED and fold its WHOLE area into `contained_area`, a LIE the moment any of its
//! pixels sits in the concavity. This rung classifies by the pixel arbiter instead, so `contained_area` stays honestly EXACT by DEMOTING
//! the concavity-carved object to CLIPPED, where its whole area over-counts openly. Two facts on metal say it:
//!
//!   THE CONCAVITY MOVES AREA FROM CONTAINED TO CLIPPED -- over the notched-square lasso, a bar Q whose whole box and both box corners lie
//!   inside the loop is CLIPPED (its middle in the slot), so its area (7) lands in `clipped_area`, NEVER in `contained_area`. A box-based
//!   digest (region_summary's method) would have folded Q's 7 into `contained_area`, inflating it to a value a pixel scan of the
//!   truly-contained objects could never confirm. The pixel arbiter keeps `contained_area` (8, the ring P held whole) EXACT against a full
//!   pixel scan, exactly because it refuses to count the object the box would have miscounted.
//!
//!   THE TOUCHED AREA IS AN UPPER BOUND, TIGHT ONLY WHEN NOTHING IS CLIPPED -- `clipped_area` reads Q's whole 7 while a pixel scan finds
//!   only 5 of its pixels inside the loop (its two middle pixels carved by the slot), so `touched_area` (15) exceeds the true in-poly
//!   foreground (13) by exactly the carved pixels. When a convex whole-scene lasso holds everything whole, nothing is clipped, so
//!   `touched_area` (19) equals the whole scene's foreground exactly -- the bound tight at the fixed point where window and crossing agree.
//!
//! Compose not braid. This rung OWNS only the per-class FOLD of area and holes over a polygon query. image/lasso_partition.rye owns the
//! containment set (`contained_in_poly`, the pixel arbiter over the concavity); image/lasso_select.rye owns the crossing set
//! (`select_in_poly`) and the `LassoPoly` type; image/scene_manifest.rye owns the per-object rows (`ObjectEntry`, `Manifest`,
//! `object_of`) and the area and hole count each carries. No count re-derived, no arbiter re-run past what the readings already proved.
//!
//! Everything is bounded before a byte: the classification walk runs `components` times, itself bounded by image/label.rye's
//! `label_max_pixels`; each per-class accumulator sums at most `components` whole areas, each under image/label.rye's pixel ceiling, so a
//! u32 total never overflows. A polygon with fewer than three vertices refuses `DegeneratePoly`, having read nothing; a lasso wholly off
//! the map is a valid all-OUTSIDE digest with zero area in every touched class, never a refusal; `largest_contained` refuses
//! `NoContained` by name when a window select keeps nothing. No network, no key, no funds -- the lasso status line a surface fills the
//! moment a keeper draws a loop, on our own open geometry.
//!
//!   env RYE_ZIG=vendor/zig-toolchain/zig rye/bin/rye run image/lasso_summary.rye selftest

const std = @import("std");
const assert = std.debug.assert;
const print = std.debug.print;
const label = @import("label.rye");
const qoi = @import("qoi.rye");
const contour = @import("contour.rye");
const scene_manifest = @import("scene_manifest.rye");
const lasso_select = @import("lasso_select.rye");
const lasso_partition = @import("lasso_partition.rye");

pub const lasso_summary_version = "20260819.045850";

pub const LassoSummaryError = error{
    DegeneratePoly,
    NoContained,
};

/// The whole digest of a polygon query as one small table. The three counts partition the scene (they sum to the component count).
/// `contained_area` is the EXACT in-poly foreground of the objects held whole; `clipped_area` is the WHOLE-object area of the objects held
/// in part, which strictly EXCEEDS their in-poly foreground; `contained_holed` and `clipped_holed` count the holed objects in each touched
/// class. Every field is an exact integer.
pub const LassoSummary = struct {
    contained_count: u32 = 0,
    clipped_count: u32 = 0,
    outside_count: u32 = 0,
    contained_area: u32 = 0,
    clipped_area: u32 = 0,
    contained_holed: u32 = 0,
    clipped_holed: u32 = 0,

    /// The total objects accounted for -- the invariant sum a caller cross-checks against the component count.
    pub fn object_total(self: *const LassoSummary) u32 {
        return self.contained_count + self.clipped_count + self.outside_count;
    }

    /// The whole-object area of everything the lasso CROSSES -- contained plus clipped. This is an UPPER BOUND on the true in-poly
    /// foreground, exact when nothing is clipped and strictly loose otherwise, since a clipped object contributes pixels outside the loop.
    pub fn touched_area(self: *const LassoSummary) u32 {
        return self.contained_area + self.clipped_area;
    }
};

/// TRUE when `want` appears in an ascending label set -- a bounded membership read over the sorted result.
fn is_in(set: []const u32, want: u32) bool {
    // invariant: a real query names a foreground label, never background.
    assert(want >= 1);
    // invariant: the set is no longer than a full scene's component span could be.
    assert(set.len <= @as(usize, label.label_max_pixels));
    var i: usize = 0;
    while (i < set.len) : (i += 1) {
        if (set[i] == want) return true;
    }
    return false;
}

/// Walk the manifest once and fold every object's area and hole count into its window-versus-crossing class, reading the whole DIGEST in
/// one pass. Each object is CONTAINED when every pixel lies inside the loop (lasso_partition's pixel arbiter), CLIPPED when it crosses yet
/// is not whole, OUTSIDE when it never crosses -- so a contained object is always crossing (it holds at least one inside pixel), never
/// double counted. Unlike region_summary's box test, containment here is decided by the even-odd arbiter, so a concavity-carved object
/// falls to CLIPPED rather than inflating the contained class. Refuses `DegeneratePoly` for a malformed lasso, having read nothing.
pub fn lasso_summary(allocator: std.mem.Allocator, manifest: scene_manifest.Manifest, map: *const label.LabelMap, poly: *const lasso_select.LassoPoly) !LassoSummary {
    // invariant: the manifest holds exactly one row per component.
    assert(manifest.objects.len == @as(usize, manifest.components));
    // invariant: the manifest and the map are the same scene -- one row per component, the same component count.
    assert(manifest.components == map.count);
    if (!poly.is_well_formed()) return error.DegeneratePoly;

    const crossing = try lasso_select.select_in_poly(allocator, manifest, map, poly);
    // invariant: no more objects cross the lasso than the scene holds.
    assert(crossing.len <= @as(usize, manifest.components));
    const contained = try lasso_partition.contained_in_poly(allocator, manifest, map, poly);
    // invariant: every contained object crosses -- the containment set is a subset of the crossing set.
    assert(contained.len <= crossing.len);

    var out = LassoSummary{};
    var i: usize = 0;
    while (i < manifest.objects.len) : (i += 1) {
        const entry = manifest.objects[i];
        const holed: u32 = if (entry.holes >= 1) 1 else 0;
        if (is_in(contained, entry.label)) {
            out.contained_count += 1;
            out.contained_area += entry.area;
            out.contained_holed += holed;
        } else if (is_in(crossing, entry.label)) {
            out.clipped_count += 1;
            out.clipped_area += entry.area;
            out.clipped_holed += holed;
        } else {
            out.outside_count += 1;
        }
    }
    // invariant: the three classes partition the scene -- every object lands in exactly one.
    assert(out.object_total() == manifest.components);
    // invariant: contained plus clipped is exactly the crossing set (contained is a subset of crossing).
    assert(out.contained_count + out.clipped_count == @as(u32, @intCast(crossing.len)));
    // invariant: the touched area never exceeds the whole scene's foreground -- a subset carries no more than the whole.
    assert(@as(u64, out.touched_area()) <= scene_manifest.total_area(manifest));
    return out;
}

/// The LABEL of the LARGEST object the lasso holds WHOLE -- the biggest keep a window select makes, ties broken by the earliest label.
/// Reads the containment set from the pixel arbiter (a polygon has no box shortcut), then the manifest for each area. Refuses
/// `NoContained` by name when the lasso holds nothing whole (a fact about containment, not emptiness of the scene), `DegeneratePoly` for a
/// malformed lasso. Takes the map because polygon containment needs the even-odd scan -- the box-only read region_summary used is gone.
pub fn largest_contained(allocator: std.mem.Allocator, manifest: scene_manifest.Manifest, map: *const label.LabelMap, poly: *const lasso_select.LassoPoly) !u32 {
    // invariant: the manifest holds exactly one row per component.
    assert(manifest.objects.len == @as(usize, manifest.components));
    // invariant: the manifest and the map are the same scene.
    assert(manifest.components == map.count);
    if (!poly.is_well_formed()) return error.DegeneratePoly;

    const contained = try lasso_partition.contained_in_poly(allocator, manifest, map, poly);
    if (contained.len == 0) return error.NoContained;

    var best_label: u32 = 0;
    var best_area: u32 = 0;
    var found: bool = false;
    var i: usize = 0;
    while (i < contained.len) : (i += 1) {
        const entry = try scene_manifest.object_of(manifest, contained[i]);
        // strictly greater keeps the earliest label on an area tie -- the contained set is ascending, so the earliest label wins.
        if (!found or entry.area > best_area) {
            found = true;
            best_label = entry.label;
            best_area = entry.area;
        }
    }
    // invariant: a non-empty contained set names a real winner.
    assert(found);
    // invariant: the winner is a real foreground label.
    assert(best_label >= 1);
    return best_label;
}

// --- selftest: the DECISIVE concavity-moves-area crux (a bar whose whole box and both box corners lie inside the loop is CLIPPED not
//     CONTAINED, so its area lands in clipped_area and contained_area stays exact against a pixel scan); the touched_area upper bound
//     loose by exactly the carved pixels, tight at the convex fixed point; the per-class holed fold; largest_contained the biggest window
//     keep; the three counts partition the scene; a lasso off the map is an all-outside digest; largest_contained refuses NoContained
//     when nothing is whole; a degenerate polygon refuses DegeneratePoly; determinism -------------------------------------------------

/// Mint a one-color RGBA canvas -- a local, bounded builder so this rung leans on no higher module.
fn make_canvas(garden: std.mem.Allocator, w: u32, h: u32, fill: u8) !qoi.Pixmap {
    // invariant: a canvas has real extent on both axes.
    assert(w >= 1);
    assert(h >= 1);
    const total: u64 = @as(u64, w) * @as(u64, h);
    // invariant: the canvas stays within the image module's named pixel ceiling.
    assert(total <= qoi.qoi_max_pixels);
    const pixels = try garden.alloc(u8, @as(usize, @intCast(total)) * 4);
    var i: usize = 0;
    while (i < pixels.len) : (i += 4) {
        pixels[i] = fill;
        pixels[i + 1] = fill;
        pixels[i + 2] = fill;
        pixels[i + 3] = 255;
    }
    return qoi.Pixmap{ .width = w, .height = h, .channels = 4, .colorspace = 0, .pixels = pixels };
}

/// Set one foreground pixel at (x, y).
fn set_dot(img: *qoi.Pixmap, x: u32, y: u32) void {
    // invariant: the write stays inside the buffer on both axes.
    assert(x < img.width);
    assert(y < img.height);
    const di = (@as(usize, y) * @as(usize, img.width) + @as(usize, x)) * 4;
    img.pixels[di] = 255;
    img.pixels[di + 1] = 255;
    img.pixels[di + 2] = 255;
    img.pixels[di + 3] = 255;
}

/// Set one pixel back to background at (x, y) -- carve a hole in a filled shape.
fn clear_dot(img: *qoi.Pixmap, x: u32, y: u32) void {
    // invariant: the write stays inside the buffer on both axes.
    assert(x < img.width);
    assert(y < img.height);
    const di = (@as(usize, y) * @as(usize, img.width) + @as(usize, x)) * 4;
    img.pixels[di] = 0;
    img.pixels[di + 1] = 0;
    img.pixels[di + 2] = 0;
    img.pixels[di + 3] = 255;
}

/// Paint a solid `bw` x `bh` foreground block with its top-left corner at (x, y).
fn set_block(img: *qoi.Pixmap, x: u32, y: u32, bw: u32, bh: u32) void {
    // invariant: the block stays inside the buffer on both axes.
    assert(x + bw <= img.width);
    assert(y + bh <= img.height);
    var yy: u32 = y;
    while (yy < y + bh) : (yy += 1) {
        var xx: u32 = x;
        while (xx < x + bw) : (xx += 1) set_dot(img, xx, yy);
    }
}

/// Count the foreground pixels of `want` that lie INSIDE the polygon by the even-odd arbiter -- the ground-truth in-poly foreground,
/// against which the whole-area sum is proven exact for CONTAINED objects and strictly larger for CLIPPED ones.
fn pixels_in_poly(map: *const label.LabelMap, poly: *const lasso_select.LassoPoly, want: u32) u32 {
    // invariant: a real query names a foreground label, never background.
    assert(want >= 1);
    // invariant: the arbiter is asked only of a well-formed lasso -- a real closed area.
    assert(poly.verts.len >= 3);
    var count: u32 = 0;
    var y: u32 = 0;
    while (y < map.height) : (y += 1) {
        var x: u32 = 0;
        while (x < map.width) : (x += 1) {
            if (map.at(x, y) == want and lasso_select.point_in_poly(poly, x, y)) count += 1;
        }
    }
    return count;
}

fn run_selftest(garden: std.mem.Allocator) !u8 {
    // --- THE PARTITION SCENE -- three objects on a 13x13 field, placed so raster order gives dense labels 1..3. R an OUTSIDE block at
    //     x[5,6] y[2,3] (it sits in the slot, area 4) [label 1]; Q a CLIPPED bar y=5 x[3,9] (ends in the arms, middle in the slot, area 7)
    //     [label 2]; P a CONTAINED 3x3 ring at x[3,5] y[8,10] (below the slot, one hole, area 8) [label 3] -----------------------------
    var scene = try make_canvas(garden, 13, 13, 0);
    set_block(&scene, 5, 2, 2, 2); // R: outside block, box (5,2)-(6,3), area 4 -- lives in the slot
    set_block(&scene, 3, 5, 7, 1); // Q: clipped bar, box (3,5)-(9,5), area 7 -- ends inside, middle in the slot
    set_block(&scene, 3, 8, 3, 3); // P: 3x3 ring...
    clear_dot(&scene, 4, 9); // ...hole carved, box (3,8)-(5,10), area 8, one hole -- below the slot

    const map = try label.label_components(garden, &scene);
    if (map.count != 3) return error.SceneNotThreeComponents;
    const manifest = try scene_manifest.build_manifest(garden, &map);
    if (scene_manifest.object_count(manifest) != 3) return error.ManifestNotThreeObjects;

    // confirm the dense labels and areas landed where the raster order predicts: R=1 area 4, Q=2 area 7, P=3 holed area 8
    const r_entry = try scene_manifest.object_of(manifest, 1);
    const q_entry = try scene_manifest.object_of(manifest, 2);
    const p_entry = try scene_manifest.object_of(manifest, 3);
    if (r_entry.area != 4) return error.RNotAreaFour;
    if (q_entry.min_x != 3 or q_entry.max_x != 9 or q_entry.min_y != 5 or q_entry.max_y != 5 or q_entry.area != 7) return error.QNotAtExpectedBox;
    if (!p_entry.is_holed() or p_entry.area != 8) return error.PNotHoledAreaEight;

    // --- THE NOTCHED SQUARE -- a rectilinear concave polygon: the square (1,1)-(11,11) with a vertical SLOT cut down from the top edge,
    //     x in (5,7), y in (1,7). Its bounding box is the whole square. Inside is the square MINUS the slot: a pixel with center x in
    //     {5,6} AND center y in {1..6} falls in the slot, outside the loop; every other on-map pixel of the square is inside ------------
    const notch_verts = [_]contour.Point{
        .{ .x = 1, .y = 1 },
        .{ .x = 5, .y = 1 },
        .{ .x = 5, .y = 7 },
        .{ .x = 7, .y = 7 },
        .{ .x = 7, .y = 1 },
        .{ .x = 11, .y = 1 },
        .{ .x = 11, .y = 11 },
        .{ .x = 1, .y = 11 },
    };
    const notch = lasso_select.LassoPoly{ .verts = notch_verts[0..] };
    if (!notch.is_well_formed()) return error.NotchNotWellFormed;

    // --- THE CONCAVITY MOVES AREA FROM CONTAINED TO CLIPPED -- the DECISIVE crux. Q's whole bounding box (3,5)-(9,5) lies inside the
    //     polygon's box (1,1)-(11,11), AND both of Q's box corners (3,5) and (9,5) lie inside the loop, so a box-approximation -- the EXACT
    //     arbiter for a rectangle in region_summary -- would call Q CONTAINED and fold its area 7 into contained_area. The pixel arbiter
    //     instead finds Q's middle in the slot and demotes Q to CLIPPED, so its area lands in clipped_area and contained_area stays exact -
    const pbox = notch.bbox(); // the polygon's own bounding box, [min_x, min_y, max_x, max_y]
    if (!(pbox[0] <= q_entry.min_x and q_entry.max_x <= pbox[2] and pbox[1] <= q_entry.min_y and q_entry.max_y <= pbox[3])) return error.QBoxNotInsidePolyBox;
    if (!lasso_select.point_in_poly(&notch, q_entry.min_x, q_entry.min_y)) return error.QCornerLoNotInside;
    if (!lasso_select.point_in_poly(&notch, q_entry.max_x, q_entry.max_y)) return error.QCornerHiNotInside;

    const s = try lasso_summary(garden, manifest, &map, &notch);
    // the notched square CONTAINS {P}, CLIPS {Q}, leaves {R} OUTSIDE -- one object in each class
    if (s.contained_count != 1 or s.clipped_count != 1 or s.outside_count != 1) return error.CountsNotchNotOneOneOne;
    if (s.object_total() != 3) return error.TotalNotchNotThree;
    // Q's area landed in clipped_area, NEVER in contained_area -- the concavity moved it despite the box test
    if (s.contained_area != 8) return error.ContainedAreaNotchNotEight;
    if (s.clipped_area != 7) return error.ClippedAreaNotchNotSeven;
    // the per-class holed fold -- P's hole counts in the contained class, Q carries none
    if (s.contained_holed != 1 or s.clipped_holed != 0) return error.HoledFoldNotchWrong;
    if (s.touched_area() != 15) return error.TouchedAreaNotchNotFifteen;

    // --- THE CONTAINED AREA IS EXACT, THE CLIPPED AREA OVER-COUNTS -- proven on metal by a full pixel scan. contained_area (8) equals P's
    //     in-poly foreground exactly (every pixel inside), while Q's whole area (7) strictly EXCEEDS its in-poly foreground (5 of its 7
    //     pixels, the two middle carved by the slot) -- the box-is-a-prefilter over-count, now at the level of area -----------------------
    const contained_pixels = pixels_in_poly(&map, &notch, 3);
    if (contained_pixels != s.contained_area) return error.ContainedAreaNotExactByPixels;
    const q_in_poly = pixels_in_poly(&map, &notch, 2);
    if (q_in_poly != 5) return error.QInPolyNotFive;
    if (!(s.clipped_area > q_in_poly)) return error.ClippedAreaNotStrictOverCount;
    // touched_area (15) is an UPPER BOUND -- the true in-poly foreground is 8 + 5 = 13, the gap exactly Q's carved pixels
    const true_in_poly = contained_pixels + q_in_poly;
    if (true_in_poly != 13) return error.TrueInPolyNotThirteen;
    if (!(s.touched_area() > true_in_poly)) return error.TouchedNotUpperBound;

    // --- LARGEST CONTAINED -- over the whole keeps {P(8)} the biggest window keep is P (label 3) -----------------------------------
    const big = try largest_contained(garden, manifest, &map, &notch);
    if (big != 3) return error.LargestContainedNotchNotP;

    // --- THE FIXED POINT -- a convex whole-scene lasso holds every object whole, nothing clipped, so touched_area equals the whole
    //     scene's foreground exactly -- the bound tight where window and crossing agree -----------------------------------------------
    const whole_verts = [_]contour.Point{
        .{ .x = 0, .y = 0 },
        .{ .x = 12, .y = 0 },
        .{ .x = 12, .y = 12 },
        .{ .x = 0, .y = 12 },
    };
    const whole = lasso_select.LassoPoly{ .verts = whole_verts[0..] };
    const sw = try lasso_summary(garden, manifest, &map, &whole);
    if (sw.contained_count != 3 or sw.clipped_count != 0 or sw.outside_count != 0) return error.CountsWholeNotThreeZeroZero;
    // whole scene foreground = R(4) + Q(7) + P(8) = 19
    if (sw.contained_area != 19 or sw.clipped_area != 0) return error.AreaWholeNotNineteenZero;
    if (sw.touched_area() != @as(u32, @intCast(scene_manifest.total_area(manifest)))) return error.TouchedWholeNotWholeScene;
    if (sw.contained_holed != 1) return error.HoledWholeNotOne;

    // --- ALL CLIPPED, NONE WHOLE -- a small convex quad over P's left columns x[0,4] y[7,11] crosses P (columns x=3,4 inside, x=5 outside)
    //     yet holds it only in part, and misses Q (y=5) and R (y=2): contained empty, clipped {P}, so contained_area is zero and
    //     largest_contained refuses NoContained -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
    const clip_verts = [_]contour.Point{
        .{ .x = 0, .y = 7 },
        .{ .x = 4, .y = 7 },
        .{ .x = 4, .y = 11 },
        .{ .x = 0, .y = 11 },
    };
    const clip = lasso_select.LassoPoly{ .verts = clip_verts[0..] };
    const sc = try lasso_summary(garden, manifest, &map, &clip);
    if (sc.contained_count != 0 or sc.clipped_count != 1 or sc.outside_count != 2) return error.CountsClipNotZeroOneTwo;
    if (sc.contained_area != 0) return error.ContainedAreaClipNotZero;
    if (sc.clipped_area != 8) return error.ClippedAreaClipNotEight;
    if (sc.clipped_holed != 1) return error.ClippedHoledClipNotOne;
    if (largest_contained(garden, manifest, &map, &clip)) |_| return error.NoContainedNotRefused else |err| {
        if (err != error.NoContained) return error.WrongNoContainedError;
    }

    // --- A LASSO OFF THE MAP -- a triangle drawn wholly past the canvas holds no object whole and crosses none: a valid all-OUTSIDE
    //     digest, every touched class zero, never a refusal --------------------------------------------------------------------------
    const off_verts = [_]contour.Point{
        .{ .x = 20, .y = 20 },
        .{ .x = 25, .y = 20 },
        .{ .x = 22, .y = 25 },
    };
    const off = lasso_select.LassoPoly{ .verts = off_verts[0..] };
    const s_off = try lasso_summary(garden, manifest, &map, &off);
    if (s_off.contained_count != 0 or s_off.clipped_count != 0 or s_off.outside_count != 3) return error.OffNotAllOutside;
    if (s_off.touched_area() != 0) return error.OffTouchedNotZero;

    // --- A DEGENERATE POLYGON -- two vertices enclose no area: every read refuses DegeneratePoly, having touched nothing ------------
    const line_verts = [_]contour.Point{
        .{ .x = 1, .y = 1 },
        .{ .x = 5, .y = 5 },
    };
    const line = lasso_select.LassoPoly{ .verts = line_verts[0..] };
    if (line.is_well_formed()) return error.DegenerateClaimedWellFormed;
    if (lasso_summary(garden, manifest, &map, &line)) |_| return error.BadSummaryNotRefused else |err| {
        if (err != error.DegeneratePoly) return error.WrongDegenerateSummaryError;
    }
    if (largest_contained(garden, manifest, &map, &line)) |_| return error.BadLargestNotRefused else |err| {
        if (err != error.DegeneratePoly) return error.WrongDegenerateLargestError;
    }

    // --- DETERMINISM -- the same lasso reads the same digest twice -------------------------------------------------------------
    const d1 = try lasso_summary(garden, manifest, &map, &notch);
    const d2 = try lasso_summary(garden, manifest, &map, &notch);
    if (d1.contained_area != d2.contained_area or d1.clipped_area != d2.clipped_area or d1.touched_area() != d2.touched_area()) return error.SummaryNotDeterministic;

    print("GREEN image-lasso-summary: read a query POLYGON (a free-form LASSO) as a small DIGEST TABLE, joining lasso_partition's three window-versus-crossing classes (CONTAINED, CLIPPED, OUTSIDE) with scene_manifest's per-object area and holes, exact integers, NO float and NO tolerance -- the free-form companion of region_summary. The DECISIVE crux lifts the box-is-a-prefilter lesson from the OBJECT to its AREA one shape freer, where the concavity now moves AREA between classes: region_summary classified by the tight bounding box, the EXACT arbiter for a RECTANGLE, so its contained_area was exact by construction, yet for a CONCAVE LASSO that box test BREAKS -- a slot can carve pixels from the middle of an object whose whole box and both box corners lie inside the loop, so a box-based digest would fold that object's WHOLE area into contained_area, a LIE the moment any pixel sits in the concavity. This rung classifies by the even-odd PIXEL arbiter instead, keeping contained_area honestly EXACT by DEMOTING the concavity-carved object to CLIPPED. Over a notched-square lasso holding a ring P whole (area 8), a bar Q whose whole box (3,5)-(9,5) and both box corners lie inside the loop yet whose middle x=5,6 sits in the slot (area 7), and a block R in the slot (area 4): a box-approximation would call Q CONTAINED and inflate contained_area to a value a pixel scan could never confirm, yet lasso_summary reads contained_area 8 which a full pixel scan confirms is exactly P's in-poly foreground, demotes Q to clipped_area 7 though a pixel scan finds only 5 of Q's pixels inside (its two middle carved by the slot), so touched_area reads 15 where the true in-poly foreground is 13, the gap exactly Q's carved pixels; a convex whole-scene lasso holds all three whole so touched_area (19) equals the true in-poly foreground exactly, the bound tight at the no-clip fixed point; a quad over P's left columns holds none whole so contained_area is zero and largest_contained refuses NoContained; the per-class holed fold counts P's hole in whichever class holds it; the three counts partition the scene; largest_contained names P the biggest window keep; a lasso off the map is a valid all-outside digest; a two-vertex degenerate polygon refuses DegeneratePoly having read nothing; the same lasso reads the same digest twice. Compose not braid over image/lasso_partition.rye (the containment set and pixel arbiter) and image/scene_manifest.rye (the per-object area and holes)\n", .{});
    return 0;
}

pub fn main(init: std.process.Init) !u8 {
    const garden = init.arena.allocator();
    const args = try init.minimal.args.toSlice(garden);
    if (args.len >= 2 and std.mem.eql(u8, args[1], "selftest")) {
        return run_selftest(garden);
    }
    print("usage: lasso_summary selftest\n", .{});
    return 1;
}
